    "summary": "Intense playing with a legendary Playin' jam and Grace Slick cameo",
    "blurb": "The show delivers a powerhouse first set highlighted by Box of Rain and a fiery Playing In The Band. The second set shines with an electric Playin' jam featuring Grace Slick, tight transitions into The Other One > Wharf Rat, and a jubilant Sugar Magnolia.",
    "review": "Winterland on October 9‑1972 captures the Dead at a high‑octane peak. Jerry’s guitar is in top form – intense, crystal‑clear and backed by solid vocals that cut through the mix. Keith adds some of his wildest improvisations, spiraling jazzy fills throughout the Playin' jam, while Bob Weir slashes lead lines that lock with the rhythm section. The setlist rolls from classic rockers like Box of Rain and Truckin' into a soaring second‑set sequence: a seamless transition from The Other One into Wharf Rat, then a jubilant Sugar Magnolia that feels fresh despite its familiarity. A historic moment arrives when Grace Slick drops in for a brief but electrifying “Slick Jam,” turning the night into a true legend. Recording quality is good – the soundboard tape retains crisp balance with only minor hiss and a tiny cut at the start of the Playin' jam, making this version the most enjoyable listen.\n\nOverall, the performance is strong throughout, though one source notes modest compression artifacts that slightly dull quieter passages. Even with those quirks, the musical highlights – especially the intense Playing In The Band stretch and Grace Slick’s cameo – make this Winterland show a must‑listen for fans seeking late‑'72 energy.",
